course: biology
lesson: 368
title: Big wild animals in cities: wild boar, deer, coyotes, monkeys and leopards
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Cities are not only for people: wild boar dig up parks in Berlin, deer bow to tourists in Japan, coyotes cross roads in Chicago and leopards hunt at night inside Mumbai.
explain: For an animal, a city is a giant larder with warm corners: overflowing bins, fruit trees, bird feeders, and hardly any hunters.
explain: Picture a bin tipped over in the morning with rubbish all over the pavement: a fox, a boar or a bear came for an easy meal, and it will come back.
explain: Living side by side works when people follow simple rules: do not feed wild animals, keep bins shut, give mothers with babies lots of room, never stare at monkeys, and walk calmly away.
fact: Thousands of wild boar live in and around Berlin, digging up parks, gardens and graveyards for roots and grubs, because cities give them food and no hunters.
fact: In 2020 a boar ran off with a man's bag and laptop at a Berlin lake, and he chased it across the beach.
fact: A mother boar usually has about 4 to 6 striped piglets whose stripes hide them in the forest and fade after a few months, and she can charge if she thinks they are in danger.
fact: About 1,200 sika deer roam freely in Nara, Japan, as protected sacred animals; many have learned to bob their heads for special crackers, and they sometimes butt people who tease them.
fact: Richmond Park in London has about 600 red and fallow deer; in autumn the stags roar and fight, and keepers ask people to stay at least 50 metres away and keep dogs on leads.
fact: Stags grow new antlers every year, so the number of points can change from year to year.
fact: A few thousand coyotes are thought to live in and around Chicago; they eat mice, rabbits, fruit and rubbish, are mostly out at night, and some wait for gaps in traffic before crossing roads.
fact: In Lopburi, Thailand, thousands of macaque monkeys live around old temples and streets, there is a yearly monkey buffet festival, and the monkeys grab bags, phones and glasses.
fact: In many monkeys a long stare or showing your teeth is a threat, not a smile, so the safe rule is no staring, no feeding, no touching, and walk calmly away; a monkey bite needs a doctor.
fact: Sanjay Gandhi National Park inside Mumbai has around 50 leopards that hunt stray dogs and rats at night; keeping rubbish clean, lights on and children indoors after dark made attacks fall.
fact: In parts of North America and Romania bears come into towns for bins, fruit trees and bird feeders, and bear-proof bins with clever locks keep them out.
fact: A bear used to human food can lose its fear of people, which is dangerous for both, so people say a fed bear is a dead bear.
fact: Animals that learn fast and eat almost anything do best in cities, and scientists have found many city animals are bolder than their country cousins.
exercise: What do wild boar dig for in Berlin's parks and gardens? | Roots and grubs | Gold coins buried long ago | Water pipes to drink from | Lost toys
exercise: Why do wild boar like living in Berlin? | Food from bins and gardens and no hunters | The weather is much hotter than in the forest | They like the sound of trains | People give them beds
exercise: What did a boar do at a Berlin lake in 2020? | Ran off with a man's bag and laptop | Swam across the whole lake with a child | Ate a whole picnic table | Pulled a boat to shore
exercise: About how many piglets does a mother boar usually have? | About 4 to 6 | Just 1 | About 30 in one go | About 100
exercise: What do a wild boar piglet's stripes do? | Hide it in the forest | Warn that it is poisonous | Show how old it is in years | Keep it warm
exercise: What should you do if you see a mother boar with piglets? | Give her lots of room | Try to stroke a piglet | Run between her and the piglets | Feed them bread
exercise: About how many sika deer roam freely in Nara, Japan? | About 1,200 | About 12 | About 1 million | None
exercise: Why do many Nara deer bob their heads to people? | They learned it gets them special crackers | They are saying sorry for pushing people | They are trying to sleep | They have sore necks from carrying their heavy antlers
exercise: How far away do keepers ask people to stay from Richmond Park deer? | At least 50 metres | Close enough to stroke them | About 1 metre | It does not matter
exercise: When do the stags in Richmond Park roar and fight? | In autumn | In early spring | Only at Christmas | Never, they are tame
exercise: When are city coyotes mostly out and about? | At night | At midday | Only on Sundays | Only in winter snow
exercise: What do city coyotes eat? | Mice, rabbits, fruit and rubbish | Only grass | Only people's cats and nothing else | Only fish from the lake
exercise: Where in Thailand do thousands of monkeys live around old temples, with a yearly monkey buffet festival? | Lopburi | Nara | Berlin | Chicago
exercise: To many monkeys, what does a long stare or showing your teeth mean? | A threat | A friendly smile | A request for food | Nothing at all
exercise: What do many of the Mumbai leopards eat at night? | Stray dogs and rats | Only fruit from the trees | Fish they catch in the sea | Grass and leaves
exercise: What made leopard attacks fall in Mumbai? | Clean rubbish, lights on and children indoors after dark | Shooting every single leopard in the whole park and the city | Putting the leopards on long leads at night | Feeding the leopards cake
exercise: What keeps bears out of town bins? | Bear-proof bins with clever locks | Painting all the bins bright red | Leaving the lids open so bears can see in | Putting honey on top
exercise: What do people mean by «a fed bear is a dead bear»? | A bear used to human food loses its fear and often has to be killed | Bears die if they eat too much food from the bins | Feeding bears makes them hibernate for ever and never wake up again | Bears only eat once a year
test: «there's boar in the park, let's throw them our sandwiches, they're cute» Why is this a bad idea? | Fed boar come back for more, dig up gardens and get bold with people | Boar cannot eat bread at all, it makes them very ill and they die quickly | Feeding boar makes them shy and they run away for ever | Boar only eat meat
test: Count them: a mother boar crosses the road with 5 piglets behind her. How many legs cross the road? | 24 | 20 | 6 | 12
test: In a cartoon, a cute deer lets a girl pat it and then follows her home like a puppy. What would a real wild deer, like a Richmond Park stag in autumn, more likely do if someone came close? | Get stressed or even charge, so people should stay 50 metres away | Lie down on the grass so the girl can stroke it | Follow her home like a puppy and sleep in her bed | Ask for crackers by bowing its head again and again like a Nara deer
test: «waved a cracker at a Nara deer then hid it lol, it went mad» Why is teasing a bad idea? | The deer may butt or bite people who tease them | The deer cannot see crackers that are held in a hand | The deer will forget about it at once | Deer love being teased and play along with the game
test: Count them: a stag has 6 points on each antler this year. How many points in all, and will it be the same next year? | 12 now, and maybe different next year because antlers grow anew | 6 now, and it stays exactly the same for the rest of its life | 12 now, and always 12 | 24 now, then none ever again
test: Why might city coyotes be more active at night than country ones? | There are fewer people and cars around at night | Coyotes cannot see anything at all in bright daylight | City lights make them sleepy in the day | Night is warmer in cities
test: «monkey's coming for my crisps, I'll just smile at it» What should you do? | Not stare or show your teeth, put food away and walk calmly away | Smile wide and show all your teeth so it knows that you are a friend | Hold the crisps up high and wave them | Grab its tail and pull it away from you
test: A monkey in Lopburi bites a tourist's hand. What should the tourist do? | See a doctor, because monkey bites need medical care | Wash it in the fountain and forget about it, bites heal fast | Bite the monkey back | Feed the monkey more so it calms down
test: «leopards in a city?? that's fake, cities are only for people» What is true? | Around 50 leopards live in a national park inside Mumbai | No leopard has ever been near a city, they only live in jungles | Leopards live only in zoos | Only in cartoons and films
test: How can people and big cats share a city safely? | Keep rubbish clean, lights on and children indoors after dark | Leave food out every night so the cats are always full | Let children play outside all night | Keep stray dogs tied up in the streets as bait for the cats
test: What would happen if a whole town used bear-proof bins? | Bears would find less food in town and come less often | Bears would learn to open the locks and drive cars | Bears would move into houses instead of bins | Nothing would change, because bears never eat rubbish
test: Which kind of animal does best in a city? | One that learns fast and eats almost anything | One that eats only one kind of leaf from one tree | One that needs total quiet | One that is scared of everything new
exam: Identify the principal reasons for which wild animals establish themselves within cities. | The abundance of food waste, warm shelter and the scarcity of hunters | The preference of wild animals for loud noise and the bright lights of streets | The provision of beds by the municipal authorities | The total absence of any food whatsoever in rural areas
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a prolonged stare directed at a monkey? | It is liable to be interpreted as a threat | It is invariably interpreted as an expression of friendship | It constitutes a request for food | It is disregarded entirely by monkeys
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the measures adopted in Mumbai in respect of leopards. | Clean disposal of refuse, lighting and keeping children indoors at night reduced attacks | The leopards were confined upon leads | The leopards were fed at public expense in the centre of the city every day | The leopards were all removed to another continent
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression «a fed bear is a dead bear». | A bear habituated to human food loses its fear and frequently must be destroyed | A bear invariably dies in consequence of overeating the food of humans | A bear which is fed hibernates permanently | A bear requires food on one occasion only in its lifetime
